{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVmoEwb6ETzBdGkTwrDz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about testing an MCP server where the tool schema recently changed. They're noting that a passing type check doesn't guarantee the client can still call the tool. This is a great question about the gap between static verification and runtime correctness.\n\nLet me think through this:\n\n1. **What needs to be tested**: \n   - The MCP server needs to actually be running\n   - A real MCP client needs to connect to it\n   - The client needs to discover the tool (request tools list from server)\n   - The client needs to actually call the tool with the new schema\n   - The server needs to handle the call correctly\n   - The response needs to come back in the expected format\n\n2. **Why type checking isn't enough**:\n   - Type checking happens at build time and only validates your code's internal consistency\n   - It doesn't test the wire protocol (how JSON is serialized/deserialized)\n   - It doesn't test what a real client sees when it connects\n   - Schema changes could affect how arguments are passed over the wire\n   - The server's tool definition (schema declaration) could be out of sync with what it actually handles\n\n3. **End-to-end flow**:\n   - Start the MCP server (probably via stdio or network transport)\n   - Have a client connect (using MCP client SDK or `npx @modelcontextprotocol/cli connect-server`)\n   - Call the `list_tools` RPC to see what the server advertises\n   - Call the tool with a test invocation matching the new schema\n   - Verify the response is correct\n   - Optionally call with edge cases or error conditions\n\nThe user is asking \"what would you have to actually drive\" - they want to know:\n1. What are the concrete things to test/verify\n2. How does the testing flow work end-to-end\n\nI should explain:\n- The server needs to be running (can't just do static analysis)\n- A client needs to connect and interact\n- The advertised schema must match what the server actually accepts when invoked\n- Real request-response cycles are essential to catch serialization mismatches or argument handling issues\n- Several tools exist for this: the MCP CLI, a custom test client, or browser-based inspector tools","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7051,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7051},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"9672c5be-209f-4bf6-8fd0-0ddef2686aea","timestamp":"2026-07-29T05:10:43.473Z","request_id":"req_011CdVmoE2GpLNX83x47aPLa"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVmoEwb6ETzBdGkTwrDz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You need to **actually run the server and have a client call it**. Type checking only verifies your code's internal consistency—it doesn't catch mismatches between what you *advertise* (the schema the client sees) and what you *handle*, and it doesn't test the wire protocol.\n\nHere's what must happen end-to-end:\n\n1. **Start the server** — spawn it with its actual transport (stdio, SSE, or socket)\n2. **Connect a client** — use the MCP SDK or CLI to connect to that running instance\n3. **Discover tools** — client calls `list_tools` RPC; verify the schema matches your changes\n4. **Call the tool** — invoke it with arguments matching the new schema shape\n5. **Verify the response** — confirm it returns the right format and values\n\n**Why type checking fails here:**\n- Schema is often defined as a data structure (JSON or dictionary), not statically typed code. Changes to its shape aren't caught by TypeScript.\n- The tool handler might not actually validate the arguments it receives—it'll accept anything if you're not careful.\n- Serialization/deserialization over JSON can hide type mismatches (extra fields dropped, missing fields unnoticed).\n\n**How to drive it:**\n\nThe easiest path depends on your stack:\n- **`npx @modelcontextprotocol/cli connect-server`** — CLI for connecting to a local server, listing tools, and making calls interactively\n- **MCP SDK client** — write a small Node.js/Python script that connects and calls the tool (the SDK handles the RPC plumbing)\n- **Instrumented test** — spin up the server in a test fixture, connect a real client, make calls, assert responses\n\nWould you want me to help you set one of these up for your server? What transport does it use (stdio, SSE, etc.) and what language is it written in?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,